; THE SCHEDULE BUFFER REVERSED  round key by round key  so that the SAME
; conveyor feeds a decryption;
;
; FIPS 197 section 5 point 3 consumes the round keys last to first; the
; buffer holds them first to last  because that is the order they are made
; in and block/rkappend208 can only append; A second conveyor reading from
; the far end cannot be written at all: the far end MOVES as the buffer
; empties  so its address would be a computed offset  which is the one
; thing the conveyor rule exists to forbid; So the buffer is turned round
; once  here  and block/rkconsume208 then runs unchanged;
;
; ONE CONSUMER IN THE LIBRARY  which is what this is for; a decryption is
; then the same text as an encryption apart from the round body: both take
; sixteen bytes off the front and slide; A mirrored consumer would have been
; the same number of lines and would have left two routines to keep right;
;
; IT IS CHEAP AND IT IS ONCE; six swaps of sixteen bytes is
; two hundred and eighty eight moves  against a decryption whose conveyor costs that
; much EVERY round  thirteen times over; The middle group does not move and the
; arithmetic says so rather than a special case: with thirteen groups there are
; six pairs and one left over;
;
; THE BYTES INSIDE A ROUND KEY DO NOT TURN  only the groups do; A reversal
; carried all the way down to the byte would hand each round a key with its
; bytes backwards  and produce sixteen plausible bytes at the end; No
; contract here can see that; only a vector can  which is why the published
; ones go both ways;
;
; THE SCRATCH IS THE BLOCK'S OWN  sixteen cells immediately above the
; buffer  claimed clear at both ends;
;
; CONTRACTS ARE RELATIVE and the include's offset resolves them;
;
; TAPE MAP  (relative to the block's own zero)
;   @0x000:0x0cf  the schedule buffer{208}  turned round in place
;   @0x0d0:0x0df  scratch{16}  one round key in transit
;
; ASSERT ptr=+0
; ASSERT zero +208:+223

